23:03:17 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: A government (specifically its central bank or monetary authority) doesn’t physically track every single note and coin in real time—there’s no giant GPS on each bill. Instead, it **accounts for physical money through precise record-keeping of issuance and returns**. Here’s how they get the “exact” amount at any point: ### 1. Physical money is a liability of the central bank When a central bank prints banknotes or mints coins, it issues them into the economy. Because it creates them, it knows exactly how many have been produced and at what face value. That total is recorded on the central bank’s balance sheet as “currency issued.” ### 2. Subtract what’s still inside the banking system Not all issued cash is in the hands of the public. A portion is sitting in: - **Central bank vaults** (their own holdings), - **Commercial bank vaults** (cash they hold to meet day‑to‑day needs). Commercial banks report their vault cash regularly (often daily) to the central bank. So the formula in practice is: > **Currency in circulation = Total currency issued – cash held by the central bank – cash held by commercial banks** That number is updated frequently and gives the amount of physical money that has left the banking system and is circulating among people and businesses. It’s an accounting identity, not a physical headcount. ### 3. Why it’s “exact” (from an accounting standpoint) Central banks have airtight records of every note they’ve ever shipped out and every note that comes back (for destruction or recirculation). When a commercial bank deposits worn‑out bills, the central bank removes them from circulation and updates its totals. The difference—notes not yet returned—is by definition the amount still out there. That figure can be known to the last unit at the close of each business day. ### 4. Caveats – the fuzziness of “physical” reality While the *accounting* number is exact, the *physical* location isn’t. Some notes are lost, destroyed in fires, hoarded under mattresses, or taken abroad and never return. The central bank doesn’t adjust the “in circulation” figure for those unknowns; it only adjusts when notes are physically redeemed or replaced. So from the public’s perspective, the “exact amount in circulation” is a net supply figure that includes cash that may no longer exist but hasn’t been officially returned. --- **In short:** Governments know the exact amount because every piece of physical money is born on their balance sheet, and they log every piece that comes back. The amount that’s “out there” is simply the unreturned remainder—a precise number backed by strict issuance and deposit records.
